  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather continues into the middle of this week \nwith above average temperatures.\n\nHigh pressure gradually shifts offshore Tuesday as upper-level \nridging builds across the eastern US. This will result in dry \nconditions and a warming trend through midweek. Mostly sunny skies \nare expected for Tuesday with highs ranging from the lower to mid \n70s. Temperatures warm further Wednesday as low level flow becomes \ntakes on a southerly component, with highs reaching the mid to upper \n70s across much of the area. A few locations across interior \nsouthern VA and northeast NC could approach 80 degrees. Temperatures \nwill likely be somewhat cooler along the immediate coastline due to \nan onshore wind. \n\nSkies gradually clear out this evening into tonight, though some \npatchy fog cannot be ruled out as we approach sunrise Tuesday AM. \nTemperatures drop back into the 40s for most areas tonight. Mostly \nclear skies and light winds are expected Tuesday night with lows \ngenerally in the 40s to lower 50s. Increasing moisture and clouds \nahead of the next system should result in a milder Wednesday night, \nwith lows mainly in the 50s to around 60.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances increase Thursday into Friday, with the \ngreatest chances likely Friday. Locally heavy rainfall is possible, \nalthough uncertainty remains regarding rainfall amounts and \nplacement.\n\nThe primary forecast challenge for later this week continues to \nrevolve around the timing of an approaching upper trough and \nassociated cold front. Latest 12z deterministic guidance has come \ninto slightly better agreement, with both the GFS and ECMWF starting \nto favor Friday as the primary period for precipitation. Of note, \nthe GFS has now trended a bit slower with the upper trough compared \nto previous runs. \n\nWhile some showers still look possible on Thursday into Thursday \nnight, the more widespread precipitation appears increasingly likely \nto occur Friday as the front slowly progresses across the area. Both \nthe 12z GFS and ECMWF depict a more substantial rainfall signal \nFriday, particularly across eastern and central portions of the \narea. The ECMWF even depicts localized QPF maxima exceeding 2 \ninches. However, considerable uncertainties remains regarding the \nexact placement and magnitude of the heavier precipitation, \nespecially given the potentially convective nature of the rainfall. \n\nAlthough confidence is increasing Friday being the primary \nprecipitation period, ensemble guidance has been less aggressive \nwith rainfall amounts compared to some of the deterministic \nsolutions. Thus, would be hesitant to place too much emphasis on \nthe localized QPF maximum at this time. Still, the potentially for \nlocally heavy rainfall will at least need to monitored, particularly \nfor E/SE portions of the area. In addition, a few thunderstorms will \nbe possible Thursday night into Friday, although the degree of \ninstability remains uncertain. \n\nTemperatures will remain above normal ahead of the front, with only \nmodest cooling expected behind it. Generally drier conditions are \nexpected for the weekend, although some uncertainty remains \nregarding the progression of the front/potential lingering rain \nalong the immediate coast.",
